#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:3
msgid "Design Philosophy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:5
msgid ""
"This page describes what LLVM-libc is for, who it serves, and what it "
"deliberately does not try to be. Most of the rules elsewhere in the "
"developer documentation follow from the decisions recorded here. Read this "
"page before starting a change whose goal is \"make program X work with LLVM-"
"libc\", because the easiest way to reach that goal is often the wrong one "
"for the project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:11
msgid "An application libc"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:13
msgid ""
"Most C libraries are *system* libcs. One shared copy is installed on a "
"machine, every program on that machine uses it, and so its ABI and "
"observable behavior are very hard to change. LLVM-libc is designed as an "
"*application* libc: it is built for a specific program and linked into it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:18
msgid "This has consequences throughout the project:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:20
msgid ""
"**Static linking is the primary use case.** Dynamic loading is under "
"development because features such as `dlopen` need it, but it is not the "
"primary use case and should not drive design decisions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:23
msgid ""
"**There is no stable ABI.** The library and the program are built together, "
"so type layouts and internal symbols are free to change. See {ref}"
"`abi_policy`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:26
msgid ""
"**The library is configured for its user.** Each target selects its own set "
"of entrypoints, configuration options, and generated headers, instead of the "
"library shipping one build that tries to suit everyone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:29
msgid ""
"**Users track `main`.** There is no separate release stream for LLVM-libc "
"today. This is likely to change when the library is shipped more widely."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:32
msgid ""
"Writing a new libc is an opportunity to design an API and ABI for modern "
"systems. Preserving the freedom to do that is worth more to the project than "
"making any one program work sooner."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:36
msgid "Who we build for"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:38
msgid ""
"LLVM-libc has several kinds of users: statically linked programs on Linux "
"servers, baremetal embedded systems, GPUs, other LLVM projects that use its "
"internals through {ref}`Hand-in-Hand <hand_in_hand>`, and general open-"
"source software. Their needs differ. In general, baremetal users prioritize "
"binary size, for example, while server users prioritize latency and "
"throughput."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:44
msgid ""
"The first choice is always a design that serves every user. When that is not "
"possible, a feature added for one group of users must not impose a cost on "
"the others that they cannot configure away."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:48
msgid ""
"The longer-term milestones for general software support are building web "
"browsers (Chromium and Firefox) and the generic Linux utilities needed for a "
"complete Linux system built with LLVM and no GNU components."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:52
msgid "Non-goals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:54
msgid ""
"**Compatibility with glibc, or any other libc, as a goal in itself.** LLVM-"
"libc implements the C and POSIX standards. It does not pretend to be another "
"implementation. See {ref}`making_existing_software_work` for what this means "
"in practice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:58
msgid ""
"**Bug-for-bug compatibility.** Where a standard defines the behavior, LLVM-"
"libc follows the standard even if a widely used libc does not. See {ref}"
"`undefined_behavior`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:61
msgid ""
"**\"POSIX everywhere\".** On platforms that are not POSIX systems, the API "
"should be shaped for the target and not be an emulation of Linux."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:64
msgid ""
"A feature that is missing today is not necessarily a non-goal. Dynamic "
"loading, a time zone database, and thread cancellation, for example, are "
"planned or in progress. {ref}`roadmap` lists the planned features and their "
"status."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:69
msgid "Text, encodings, and locales"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:71
msgid ""
"The locale design in the C standard conflates two different kinds of text: "
"text exchanged with other programs (file formats, protocols, command lines) "
"and text shown to or read from a person. Modern software needs to treat "
"these differently, and a process-wide locale that silently changes the "
"behavior of `printf` or `strtod` makes the first kind unreliable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:77
msgid "LLVM-libc's direction is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:79
msgid ""
"**Functions without an explicit locale argument ignore locales.** They "
"assume their input or output is exchanged with another program."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:81
msgid ""
"**`char` holds the platform's native encoding.** That is usually ASCII "
"compatible, but it may be EBCDIC: libc++ supports IBM targets, and LLVM-libc "
"supports libc++. Code must not assume ASCII values or that letters are "
"contiguous."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:85
msgid "**Multibyte strings are UTF-8.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:86
msgid ""
"**`wchar_t` strings are UTF-32 or UTF-16**, depending on the target. UTF-16 "
"support is in progress."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:88
msgid ""
"**Encoding assumptions are centralized.** They live in `src/__support/"
"ctype_utils.h`, `src/__support/wctype_utils.h`, and the converters in `src/"
"__support/wchar/`. All code uses those helpers instead of doing arithmetic "
"on characters, including greater/less than comparisons."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:92
msgid ""
"**Text for people goes through explicit-locale functions.** The intent is to "
"provide the `_l` variants of the relevant functions, so that a caller who is "
"formatting text for display passes a locale explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:96
msgid ""
"Today only the \"C\" locale is implemented. A function that depends on a "
"locale implements the \"C\" locale behavior and does not claim to do more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:101
msgid "Making existing software work"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:103
msgid ""
"Building real programs against LLVM-libc is how missing pieces are found, "
"and those reports are welcome. What matters is how each gap is closed. In "
"order of preference:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:107
msgid ""
"**Fix LLVM-libc where it deviates from a standard.** A wrong return value or "
"a missing error condition is simply a bug."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:109
msgid "**Implement the missing standard function, macro, type, or header.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:110
msgid ""
"**Add transitive includes in public headers that POSIX requires** when a "
"real program relies on them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:112
msgid ""
"**Add a transitive include described by a standard** when a real program "
"relies on it. When a standard says that a header \"may make visible\" the "
"symbols of another header, including that header is acceptable. The "
"permission alone is not a reason to add the include. `<arpa/inet.h>` "
"includes `<netinet/in.h>` on this basis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:117
msgid ""
"**Add a widely used extension** (GNU, BSD, or Linux specific) when a real "
"program needs it. See {ref}`extensions_and_stubs`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:119
msgid ""
"**Accommodate a non-standard assumption** only when doing so is cheap and "
"does not limit future changes to LLVM-libc. Declaring `alloca` from "
"`<stdlib.h>` is an example of an acceptable accommodation. A transitive "
"include that no standard requires or permits belongs here, not under steps 3 "
"and 4. Matching the way another libc happens to lay out its headers is not a "
"sufficient reason by itself, so expect such a change to need justification."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:126
msgid ""
"**Change the program, or how it is configured,** when it depends on the "
"internals of another libc or on behavior no standard promises."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:129
msgid "The following are never acceptable ways to make a program work:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:131
msgid ""
"Changing the layout or kind of a public type solely to match another libc. "
"For example, `pthread_t` is a structure in LLVM-libc. POSIX allows that, and "
"code that treats `pthread_t` as an integer or a pointer is not portable. "
"Making it a scalar would remove the freedom to change it later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:135
msgid ""
"Changing observable behavior to match another libc's deviation from a "
"standard."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:137
msgid ""
"Adding another libc's private symbols so that objects compiled against its "
"headers link. The one narrow exception is described in {ref}`abi_policy`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:140
msgid ""
"If a task can only be completed by doing one of these, stop and raise it "
"with the maintainers instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:145
msgid "Extensions, stubs, and experimental entrypoints"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:147
msgid ""
"Before implementing a function that is not in a standard, ask whether anyone "
"needs it. An extension needs a specific user: a named program or library "
"that calls it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:151
msgid ""
"A function that is declared and linkable but not fully implemented goes "
"behind the `LLVM_LIBC_ENABLE_EXPERIMENTAL_ENTRYPOINTS` CMake option. This "
"applies to standard functions as well as extensions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:155
msgid ""
"A stub or partial implementation of a standard function may be added there "
"when the function cannot be completed yet. For this purpose the GNU "
"extensions may be treated like a standard."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:158
msgid "A stub of any other non-standard function needs a good reason."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:159
msgid ""
"In both cases, a call that the stub cannot handle fails clearly. A stub that "
"silently reports success hides the missing functionality from the people who "
"would otherwise report or fix it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:163
msgid ""
"An entrypoint leaves the experimental list when it implements all of the "
"functionality its specification requires. Some incomplete entrypoints "
"predate the option and are still in the default lists; they should move."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:169
msgid "Original implementations only"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:171
msgid ""
"Do not copy code from other libc implementations, and do not translate or "
"port it. Rewriting another libc's C code as C++ is still taking that code. "
"This is a licensing requirement, and it is also a question of style: LLVM-"
"libc is written in idiomatic C++, while most other libcs are written in C. "
"Code translated from C rarely fits the conventions described in the {ref}"
"`developer guides <developer_guides>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:178
msgid ""
"Standards documents, manual pages, and observing the behavior of another "
"implementation as a black box are all appropriate references. Knowing how "
"other implementations approach a problem is generally acceptable too, as a "
"way to learn what the options are. The code you contribute must still be "
"written from scratch and must not be based on theirs. This is not legal "
"advice. Contributors are responsible for having the right to contribute "
"their code; see LLVM's policy on [copyright, license, and patents](https://"
"llvm.org/docs/DeveloperPolicy.html#copyright-license-and-patents)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:187
msgid ""
"The same applies to code written with AI tools: it must be new code and not "
"a reproduction of existing code. See the copyright section of LLVM's [AI "
"Tool Use Policy](https://llvm.org/docs/AIToolPolicy.html)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../design_philosophy.md:191
msgid ""
"If you own an existing implementation of a libc function that you would like "
"to contribute, that is likely fine assuming it is updated to match the LLVM-"
"libc style and placed under the LLVM license. Reach out to the maintainers "
"for more information."
msgstr ""
